PUBLIC RESPONSE: I come down on the 品 side, the classic three-storey /三横二竖 sign.

The verbal inventory is

* 一横 再一横 再一横 = the three horizontal bars, top–middle–bottom;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three short vertical segments that make up the left side of the three storeys;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the left/right framing that is visible in the 品/丰/王 family;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three short vertical segments that make up the right side of the three storeys.

That is 3 horizontals + 3 + 3 vertical segments = 9 strokes, the ordinary 品 pǐn, “goods / grade / taste”, the textbook illustration of 三横两竖 framed on the left and on the right.

晶 jīng is the close folk variant that gets conflated with it. 三个日 side by side does give three top horizontals and three left and three right verticals, but it also needs three bottom horizontals, six horizontals in all, which the riddle does not enumerate. The poem here only names three horizontals in total, so the reduced nine-stroke three-bar form 品, and in its graphic shorthand 丰/王/田, is the canonical answer that the riddle is pointing to. 

The 册/冊 proposal is a pure horizontal/vertical bamboo-slip picture, but it does not give the repeated three-bar left/right framing described here.

