The inventory is being read literally as a  一/丨 inventory, not as a semantic decomposition.

* 一横 再一横 再一横 = three horizontal bars, top–middle–bottom;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three short vertical segments that make up the left side of the three storeys;
* 一竖在左 一竖在右 = the explicit left / right framing of the 丰/王/品 family;
* 一竖 再一竖 再一竖 = the three short vertical segments that make up the right side of the three storeys.

That is 3 horizontals + 6 vertical segments = the 9-stroke textbook 三横两竖 sign

品 pǐn “goods, grade, taste”

which is the graphic archetype of the 丰 fēng / 王 wáng / 田 tián family. The figure is normally taught as three 口-like storeys framed on the left and on the right.

晶 jīng = 日 日 日 is the well-known folk concretisation of the same inventory. Three 日 side by side does give a left-right frame and three tops, but it requires three bottom horizontals as well: six horizontals in total and 12 strokes. The riddle only enumerates three horizontals in total, so the literal stroke count lines up with the reduced nine-stroke abstraction 品, with 丰 as its graphic shorthand. 冊/册 is dominated by parallel verticals with binding horizontals and does not give the repeated three-bar left/right framing described here.

So on the literal enumeration the canonical target is 品, the three-storey 三横二竖 sign; 晶 is the popular three-sun rendering of the same 一–丨 pattern.

